*My case study report for the module CRDD H1200 Digital Transformation at TU Dublin: challenges, deep-technology solutions and sustainability alignment.*

## Introduction

Tuvalu is an island nation in Oceania with about 10,600 residents living on nine small islands with a total land area of about 25 km² [@tuvalucensus2025, pp. 1, 5]. About 62% of Tuvalu’s population lives in the capital, Funafuti [@tuvalucensus2025, p. 5], half of which is projected to be flooded by daily high tides by 2050 [@mcelroy2025]. This makes Tuvalu one of the first nations directly experiencing the devastating effects of climate change. It doesn’t only face the threat of losing land, but also the threat of losing its sovereignty, identity and thus its very existence. In 2022, Tuvalu pledged its Future Now project at COP27 to become the ‘world’s first digital nation’, able to preserve its culture and continue to govern even if its land becomes uninhabitable [@rothe2024]. The programme has already produced a digital twin of a tiny islet, a rewritten constitution and the country’s first submarine cable, activated in October 2025 [@accenture-nd; @kofe2023; @kofe2025].

But digitalising a nation is no small task, and it comes with a unique set of ethical, technical and social challenges. In the following sections, we will explore the digital transformation journey of Tuvalu, its challenges, proposed solutions, and alignment with sustainability goals.

## Digital Transformation Context

Digital Transformation (DT) is defined as *‘the integration of digital technology into all areas of a business, fundamentally changing how it operates and delivers value to customers’* [@busalim2026].

This definition still applies to Tuvalu, even though not directly. For Tuvalu, the ‘customers’ are all citizens on the islands and abroad, and the value at stake is not market share but the continued existence of the state. I will structure this section around the four primary factors of any transformation, business, technology, data and people [@busalim2026].

**Drivers.** The main driver is not ‘business’, but sea-level rise. The sea around Funafuti is already about 15 cm higher than thirty years ago, and much of Tuvalu’s land and critical infrastructure is expected to lie below average high tide by 2050 [@nasa2023]. This threatens Tuvalu’s recognition as a state, since the Montevideo Convention defines a state by a permanent population, a defined territory and an independent government [@rothe2024]. The COVID-19 pandemic added yet another driver: the government needed a reliable network to the outer islands for remote schooling and public services [@kofe2025].

**Business.** In 2021, minister Simon Kofe launched the *Future Now Project* [@kofe2025]. Its legal track produced a new constitution (in force since 1 October 2023), which declares Tuvalu’s statehood and maritime zones permanent regardless of the flooding [@kofe2023]. They also seek international backing for this position: the Falepili Union treaty with Australia (2023) also opens a migration pathway of 280 visas a year [@pedro2026], and (recently) in September 2026 the UN General Assembly adopted a declaration affirming continuity of statehood and stable maritime zones [@aosis2026]. The Digital Nation would allow the government to keep serving citizens from another location in a worst-case scenario [@kofe2025]. Tuvalu has also already turned one digital asset into income: its country code *.tv*. From 2001, Verisign paid a flat fee of US\$5 million a year, while reportedly earning over US\$25 million a year from the domain itself [@ligaiula2022]. This is a nice case of *digitalisation*: using digital technology to ‘change a business model and provide new revenue’ [@busalim2026, Lec 1, Slide 42].

**Technology.** Before 2020, Tuvalu relied on weather-vulnerable satellite links with very limited capacity. For example, the main government building was reported to only have a shared bandwidth of 73 Mbps [@kofe2025]. A Kacific agreement raised this to 640 Mbps in 2020, a Starlink gateway added up to 3 Gbps in 2025, and the Vaka submarine cable, activated on 24 October 2025, can scale beyond 10 Gbps [@kofe2025]. The US\$56 million cable was fully grant-funded by foreign partners [@aiffp2024, section AIFFP contribution].

**Data.** The Digital Nation consists mainly of: a *digital twin* of the land, a *Digital Ark* of cultural objects and practices, and *digitised government services* [@pedro2026]. For COP27 in 2022, Accenture built a virtual copy of the islet Te Afualiku in six weeks [@accenture-nd], and LiDAR scans of all 124 islands and islets have since been reported complete [@siosisamuels2026]. A national digital ID is planned so that every Tuvaluan, at home or abroad, can access services online [@kofe2025].

**People.** Tuvaluan identity rests on *fenua*, a concept that links land, people and culture [@rothe2024]. In community consultations in 2025 and 2026, Tuvaluans described the project mainly as a way to safeguard their culture, not to leave their islands [@pedro2026]. Note that Tuvalu has a relatively small population, and a significant portion of those live abroad [@tuvalucensus2025]. This means that change management, necessary for DT, is relatively easy to implement, as the government can reach out to the citizens on a much more personal level than in larger countries (e.g. Estonia, a nation who managed to widely implement DT into their country).

## Challenges

Busalim [-@busalim2026, Lec 1, Slide 36] names five key challenges resulting from DT: poor data quality & bias, more vulnerable digital data, resistance to digital culture, the risk of over-automation and difficult governance. The nation is making great progress, but I found 5 concrete challenges they have yet to solve. They’re labeled C1–C5 so that [the solutions section](#proposed-innovative-and-sustainable-solutions) can answer each one separately.

**Organisational.** *C1 Donor dependency.* The Vaka cable was entirely grant-funded [@aiffp2024, section AIFFP contribution]. Those donors want to be involved in the planning and implementation process: due to a World Bank spending deadline ‘the government was required to utilize funds within a specific timeframe, leading their cabinet to approve digital overhauls of health, attorney general, and immigration offices’ [@mittal2025]. Across the Pacific, donors pay for one-off infrastructure but give little attention to maintenance and local skills [@mittal2025]. This doesn’t just lead to wasted resources, but also creates a lack of control over the own projects. *C2 Talent flight.* Mittal and Ranjan [-@mittal2025] call brain drain ‘perhaps the most fundamental constraint on sustainable digital transformation’ in the region. The Falepili (treaty with Australia for gradual migration due to land loss) pathway offers 280 Australian visas a year [@pedro2026], equal to about 2.6% of Tuvalu’s residents each year [@tuvalucensus2025]. The two barriers reinforce each other: partners fund the hardware, but the people needed to run it may leave.

**Technical.** *C3 Cyber risk and uneven access.* The government itself expects the cable to bring more cyber threats, scams and cyberbullying [@kofe2025]. For a state that plans to keep its registries online, a breach would threaten sovereignty, not just privacy. Of course, state-issued real-name databases are an extremely valuable target for private as well as governmental hackers. Access is also uneven, as 4G is so far available only on Funafuti [@mittal2025]. *C4 Legal gaps and foreign hosting.* Tuvalu has neither digital ID legislation nor a data protection law [@nationalityforall2025]. At the same time, moving government services or cultural resources to the cloud ‘would create whole new dependencies’ on transnational corporations and infrastructure in other countries [@rothe2024]. Without any data protection law, Tuvalu has no legal leverage over these providers.

**Cultural.** *C5 Cultural acceptance & legitimacy.* The idea of a digital nation is heavily debated. In 2023, opposition leader Enele Sopoaga argued that a digital Tuvalu in the metaverse ‘implies that Tuvalu will disappear’, and international critics spoke of ‘technological solutionism’ [@rothe2024], which means blindly relying on technology without addressing the underlying social and political issues. Tuvaluans in community consultations support the project as a way to safeguard their culture while *staying* [@pedro2026]. If it looks like preparation for leaving, it may lose that support.

All those challenges form a cycle: donor money (C1) buys systems that few local staff can maintain (C2). This means data is stored on foreign platforms without necessary legal safeguards in place (C4), which increases cyber risk (C3) and weakens public trust (C5). Less public trust will decrease public funding and thus makes the country more dependent on donor money (C1).

## Proposed Innovative and Sustainable Solutions

Before introducing my own recommendations, I first want to highlight the proposals by Siosi Samuels in his 2026 Blog post. His 2020 ‘5-Point Plan’ proposed renegotiating the *.tv* domain deal, better internet, a digital skills programme, a blockchain-based National Digital Ledger and investing in sovereignty. He now counts four as addressed. The ledger never got past Phase 1, and he argues ‘Tuvalu was right to wait’, because blockchain was not ready ‘for the weight of a nation’ [@siosisamuels2026].

Since the challenges form a cycle, systems thinking suggests each solution weakens the others [@busalim2026, Lec 1, Slide 49].

**Exclude donors as much as possible (C1).** I propose reserving a fixed share of the *.tv* income for a maintenance and ICT staff fund, so Tuvalu pays the running costs and sets its own priorities. New systems should build on open-source ‘digital public goods’ such as OpenCRVS, which Tuvalu is already setting up as its civil registry with the Pacific Community [@mittal2025]. With no licence fees or vendor lock-in, Tuvalu can switch partners freely.

**Keep emigrants on the team (C2).** Migration to Tuvalu can’t be stopped, and shouldn’t be. But thanks to the cable, Tuvaluan IT staff in Australia could maintain government systems remotely, creating the ‘pathways for diaspora engagement’ that Mittal and Ranjan [-@mittal2025] find missing. At home, the youth workshops in 3D scanning and digital archiving [@siosisamuels2026] could grow into paid ICT traineeships. Robotic Process Automation (RPA) can handle repetitive paperwork, ‘freeing workers for higher-value roles’ [@busalim2026, Lec 1, Slide 17], but decisions like granting citizenship must stay human to avoid over-automation [@busalim2026, Lec 1, Slide 36].

**A data embassy and access for everyone (C3).** Estonia keeps copies of its key registers in a data centre in Luxembourg, where data and servers have diplomatic immunity [@microsoft2017]. It cost about €1 million to set up and €236,000 a year to run [@err2017], a small sum next to the US\$56 million cable. Tuvalu could sign such agreements with two partners, e.g. Australia and a neutral state, so no single country holds all its data, and records survive even a successful attack on Funafuti. Joining informal threat-intelligence sharing, as Palau and Micronesia already do [@mittal2025], adds early warnings at almost no cost. As 4G is limited to Funafuti, every service should also work via SMS, following the lecture’s call for ‘multi-experience access’ [@busalim2026, Lec 1, Slide 35].

**Law first, then a small blockchain (C4).** Before launching the digital ID, Tuvalu needs a data protection law and a digital ID law. They give it legal leverage over foreign cloud providers and can be written into the data embassy agreements (S3). The ID itself should issue digitally signed credentials for citizenship, birth or land rights. Blockchain should be used far more narrowly than in the paused ledger: only a hash (a digital fingerprint) of each record and of the maritime boundary coordinates is stored on a permissioned ledger run by Tuvalu and its partner states. Anyone can then verify that a record is unchanged, even after servers move abroad. Such a ledger needs no energy-hungry mining, and since it stores fingerprints instead of the whole state, it doesn’t carry ‘the weight of a nation’.

**A digital nation for staying (C5).** The project keeps support as long as it helps people stay [@pedro2026], so the digital twin has to be useful now. Solar-powered IoT sensors for water level, waves and weather could feed the LiDAR twin, while machine-learning models, combined with NASA satellite data, forecast king-tide floods and send SMS warnings. This delivers what minister Seve Paeniu asked for, ‘proper early warning and data-based and science-led policy design’ [@nasa2023], and helps plan the reclaimed land [@undp2025]. For the Digital Ark, communities should decide what is recorded and who may see it. AI could help transcribe oral histories, but Tuvaluan is a small language with little training data, so the risk of bias is high [@busalim2026, Lec 1, Slide 37]. AI should draft; elders should check.

**Feasibility.** S1, S3 and S4 are mostly legal work and cost little; S2 and S5 need staff and sensors, which S1 helps pay for. Each should start as a small pilot, following the lecture’s advice to ‘experiment, be agile, be ready to fail’ [@busalim2026, Lec 1, Slide 79], which is exactly the lesson of the paused ledger.
